Corrects my own note from the previous commit, which was better than what it
replaced but manufactured an unowned gap out of an owned, parked decision.

The original text said "Slice C will wire those to external-tier nodes". That
was wrong twice over, and my first correction only caught the first half:

1. Slice C shipped without doing it. (Caught.)
2. The symbol side was never where external references belong. (Missed.)
   Hypergumbo materialises them from the EDGE side — create_boundary_nodes
   mints external_symbol nodes for dangling edge endpoints. A SymbolInformation
   with no Definition in this index is a REFERENCE, not a definition, so a
   Symbol shim skipping it is correct rather than a gap. "Tiered" was wrong
   outright too: supply_chain.py classifies at DISCOVERY time, on files,
   before analysis (ADR-0041). No analyzer tiers anything.

Where the behaviour actually lives: translate.py passes a resolve_symbol map
that returns None for a SCIP symbol with no in-workspace definition, dropping
the edge instead of letting it dangle. That is deliberate and load-bearing.
Before 9266762d4b endpoints were raw SCIP descriptor strings, so EVERY scip
edge dangled and finalize's endpoint-integrity step silently discarded the
whole scip call graph while the Symbols survived (0 -> 739 edges on zoxide
once resolved). Reversing it is not free: it needs a canonical
{lang}:{path}:{span}:{name}:{kind} id minted from the SCIP symbol, and
WI-huzuv forbids fabricating a path from the "external" sentinel.

The residual is real and now stated with its owner rather than as a
discovery: a Rust call into a dependency or the stdlib produces no edge under
this backend, where the tree-sitter analyzers would emit one of their ~69
":unresolved" dsts. That is WI-gojum sub-component 1 ("external calls
recorded accurately"), parked by the owner 2026-07-19 pending ADR-0012
Steps 2-3, whose description already records the 9266762d4b history.

